Subject: Report a class-scope using-declaration through `ParseCallbacks`

`using Base::foo;` in a class is listed among the cursor kinds bindgen
deliberately does not handle, and a class-scope one does not even reach that
list: it falls into `CompInfo`'s catch-all and produces a `warn!` about an
unhandled comp member.

Nothing is generated for it, which is the right answer for bindgen's own
output - the declaration has no signature and no symbol, being a second name
for a member which already exists - but the fact is worth reporting, because
it changes what the derived class exposes. It is how C++ re-exports a member
of a private base, widens the access of a protected one, and un-hides an
overload set, and none of that survives into the generated Rust, where a base
is a field and its members are free functions over the base's own type. A
consumer emitting code against the derived class cannot otherwise tell that
the member is reachable through it at all.

`denote_using_declaration` reports the name and the base class it is taken
from. Purely additive: no generated code changes, and the `warn!` those
declarations used to produce goes away.

Two things libclang gets wrong here, which is why the report is a name and a
scope rather than a set of declarations. `clang_getNumOverloadedDecls` on the
using-declaration cursor answers zero; asked on its `CXCursor_OverloadedDeclRef`
child it answers with a proper subset of what the declaration introduces - one
of two overloads for `using Base::foo;`, and the copy and move constructors,
which are precisely the ones C++ does not inherit, for `using Base::Base;`. And
clang names an inherited-constructor declaration after the *derived* class
rather than the base, so `UsingDeclarationInfo::name` says so.
